  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through next Tuesday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: Dry and windy conditions over central\nand northern Nevada this afternoon and over the next few days as\na low pressure system over the northwestern coast keeps a near\nzonal flow over Nevada with quick moving storm systems staying to\nthe north. Elevated wind speeds from the west expected to reach \nup to 15-20 mph with gusts as high as 25-30 mph this afternoon, \nwhile winds over the next two days be slightly weaker at 10-15 \nmph, gusts 20-25 mph. Temperatures expected to be near or just a \nfew degrees below normal, with highs staying in the upper 50s with\novernight lows at near normal in the 20s.\n\nBy late Thursday and overnight into Friday, the low pressure\nsystem to the northwest will move south along the Pacific\ncoastline, shifting the zonal flow into a northeast flow. This\nflow will have weak dynamic forcing over northeastern Nevada which\nwill cause saturation to occur and potential for some very light\nshowers, mainly over the high mountains. By Friday afternoon, \nchances increase for more precipitation across eastern Nevada as \nthe northeast flow continues to give weak dynamic forcing with \nincrease precipitation over the mountains and reaching into the \nvalleys as PWAT amounts increases up to 0.5 inches. A slight \ncooling in temperatures is expected with highs staying just a few \ndegrees below normal in the mid 50s. \n\nSaturday, the low pressure system over the northwest continues to\nbring a northeast flow over Nevada and begin to push east, while\nat the same time, another low pressure system further south in the\nPacific will push into the southwestern CONUS with subtropical \nmoisture. Current models has the moisture from this second upper\nlevel system spread over the southwestern states and converge \ninto the first upper level system in the northwest. This will \nbring some of that subtropical moisture into Nevada that will \nincrease the chance for more precipitation across central and \nnorthern Nevada, with highest amounts expected over Northeastern \nNye and White Pine Counties. Precipitation chances are expected to\nlast into Sunday morning then begin to dissipate as the front \nfrom both systems will push eastward out of Nevada with dry \nconditions returning Monday. Cooler temperatures expected over the\nweekend as highs expected to be in the 50s, then drop to several \ndegrees below normal on Sunday with much of central and northern \nNevada reaching only in the 40s. Monday will see temperatures \nslightly rise back up but still be a few degrees below normal in \nthe low 50s.\n\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: High confidence for dry, breezy\nconditions this afternoon through Thursday afternoon. Moderate\nconfidence in regards to the northwestern low pressure system\nmoving south into Nevada with increasing chances for\nprecipitation Thursday and Friday. Moderate confidence in regards\nto a second low pressure system converging over the southwest\nwith higher chances for precipitation. Moderate confidence in \nregards to dry conditions returning Monday. No changes to the \ngrids at this time.",
